{%- comment -%}
  ITM Group Card Image
  --------------------
  Resolves the photo shown on an itm-card-product-group "Shop Range" card.
  A hub collection like "Chrome Sockets" has no products of its own and no
  image of its own either — its real content is entirely its
  product_group_children (1" Drive Chrome Sockets, 1/2" Drive Chrome
  Sockets, etc.), and THOSE can themselves be hubs with no image, one level
  further down (e.g. 1" Drive Chrome Sockets has no image but points at
  Chrome Sockets - 1" Drive Metric, which finally has real products).
  Rather than show "image coming soon" the moment the first level comes up
  empty, this walks down the first-child chain (bounded to 6 levels — the
  real tree never goes deeper) until it reaches a collection with a real
  image or a real product to borrow a photo from.

  This is a plain bounded loop, NOT a snippet rendering itself — Shopify's
  Liquid engine doesn't allow a snippet to {% render %} itself recursively,
  so walking the chain here (rather than recursing into a copy of this
  snippet) is the only way to do this in one file.

  Params:
    - collection (Collection) — required
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign current = collection -%}
{%- assign resolved_image = nil -%}
{%- assign resolved_product = nil -%}
{%- assign resolved_title = collection.title -%}
{%- assign debug_hops = '' -%}
{%- assign debug_stop_reason = '' -%}

{%- for i in (0..5) -%}
  {%- if current == blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Only record this generic reason if a more specific one wasn't
      already set at the end of the previous iteration — otherwise this
      unconditionally overwrites the real cause every time, which is
      exactly what happened last round: the log said "current became
      blank" no matter what actually caused it, telling us nothing.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if debug_stop_reason == blank -%}
      {%- assign debug_stop_reason = 'current was already blank entering iteration ' | append: i -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- break -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    current, from the second iteration on, is itself an object obtained
    via another collection's metafield reference — and a collection
    obtained that way only exposes its basic fields (title, image,
    products, handle), NOT its own metafields (confirmed: title/image/
    products all resolved fine, but product_group_children came back
    blank even on a collection verified to have it set). Re-fetching it
    through the global `collections` object by handle gets a fully native
    collection whose own metafields resolve normally.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign current = collections[current.handle] -%}
  {%- if current == blank -%}
    {%- assign debug_stop_reason = 'handle re-fetch came back blank' -%}
    {%- break -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign debug_hops = debug_hops | append: current.title | append: ' > ' -%}

  {%- if current.image -%}
    {%- assign resolved_image = current.image -%}
    {%- assign resolved_title = current.title -%}
    {%- break -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Same primary-product resolution as the card itself: the
    primary_product_ref metafield NetSuite writes first, then the older
    per-product boolean flag. After that, don't just grab
    collection.products.first blindly — the collection's own sort order can
    put a product with no photo uploaded first, while a later one in the
    same category has a perfectly good one. Scan for the first product that
    actually HAS a photo instead, and only fall back to a genuinely
    image-less product as the last resort (so there's still something for
    the CDN-guess path below, or so the card at least links somewhere).
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign primary_product = current.metafields.custom.primary_product_ref.value -%}
  {%- assign first_with_image = nil -%}
  {%- assign first_any = nil -%}
  {%- if primary_product == blank -%}
    {%- for p in current.products limit: 20 -%}
      {%- if first_any == blank -%}
        {%- assign first_any = p -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if p.metafields.custom.primary_product -%}
        {%- assign prim_val = p.metafields.custom.primary_product.value | default: p.metafields.custom.primary_product -%}
        {%- if prim_val == true or prim_val == 'true' or prim_val == 1 or prim_val == '1' -%}
          {%- assign primary_product = p -%}
          {%- break -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if first_with_image == blank and p.featured_image -%}
        {%- assign first_with_image = p -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if primary_product == blank and first_with_image -%}
    {%- assign primary_product = first_with_image -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if primary_product == blank and first_any -%}
    {%- assign primary_product = first_any -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if primary_product -%}
    {%- assign resolved_product = primary_product -%}
    {%- break -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Nothing on this level — step down into its first real child and try
    again next iteration. A list.collection_reference metafield with
    exactly ONE reference can resolve as the bare collection object itself
    rather than a one-item array (a Shopify Liquid quirk), so kids.size
    would be nil (Collection has no such property) and silently look empty
    — check for that shape explicitly rather than assuming an array.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign kids = current.metafields.custom.product_group_children.value -%}
  {%- assign next_col = nil -%}
  {%- if kids != blank -%}
    {%- if kids.size -%}
      {%- assign next_col = kids.first -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- assign next_col = kids -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if next_col == blank -%}
    {%- assign debug_stop_reason = 'no product_group_children found on: ' | append: current.title -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign current = next_col -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{%- if resolved_image -%}
  <div class="sbc__slide is-active">
    {{ resolved_image | image_url: width: 600 | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', alt: resolved_title }}
  </div>
{%- elsif resolved_product.featured_image -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Shopify's own uploaded product photo — reliable, always present when
    it exists, no dependency on the variant having a SKU or on ITM's
    external CDN actually having a matching file at a guessed path.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="sbc__slide is-active">
    {{ resolved_product.featured_image | image_url: width: 600 | image_tag: loading: 'lazy', alt: resolved_product.title }}
  </div>
{%- elsif resolved_product -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Fallback for the rarer case where Shopify has no image uploaded at all
    but the product's SKU maps to a real photo on ITM's own CDN.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign group_sku = resolved_product.selected_or_first_available_variant.sku | default: resolved_product.title | strip -%}
  <div class="sbc__slide is-active">
    <img
      src="{% render 'itm-cdn-product-image', sku: group_sku, image_number: 1 %}"
      data-cdn-fallback-src="{{ 'image_coming_soon.png' | asset_url }}"
      alt="{{ resolved_product.title | escape }}"
      width="600"
      height="600"
      loading="lazy"
    >
  </div>
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    TEMPORARY debug trail — remove once the intermittent "some cards have
    no image" issue is confirmed fixed. Shows exactly which collections
    this walked through and why it gave up, right in the page source.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <!-- itm-group-card-image: no image found. started at "{{ collection.title }}". walked: {{ debug_hops }}. stopped because: {{ debug_stop_reason }} -->
  <div class="sbc__slide is-active">
    <img src="{{ 'image_coming_soon.png' | asset_url }}" alt="{{ collection.title | escape }}" width="600" height="600" loading="lazy">
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
